title: Delta validation
tags: Schema
kind: rationale
summary: A write is checked only on the fields it touches, against the result of the write — so old violations never block unrelated work.

Only ''your writes'' are validated — creating a metarecord, writing, adding or
deleting a field row, a set write — never the daemon's own (the watcher,
reconcile). And a write checks only the ''field names it touches'', against the
metarecord as the write leaves it. By design:

* violations already on other fields of the metarecord do not block an unrelated write;
* editing or reloading the schema never scans the existing data: its violations surface through `mf schema check`, and only block a later write touching the violating field;
* writing `mf_schema` — giving or removing a type — validates only `mf_schema`, which no constraint can target, so changing a metarecord's type never fails, even if it then violates its new type's constraints: check afterwards;
* deleting a row is a write on its field: removing the last row of a field with `min ≥ 1` is refused. To remove it anyway, remove the type from the metarecord first, or relax the constraint and reload;
* deleting a ''whole metarecord'' is always allowed: cardinality does not apply to it.

On a violation the transaction is rolled back and the answer is `400` with a
`violations` array; a set write is rolled back whole.

The validation reads nothing more from the store: the parsed schema is kept in
memory, indexed by field name, and replaced at once on a reload.
